[{:db/id "q-161866/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "edc7d93b-cf32-51b8-80b9-52d26cf641a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f8ecc69b-0208-57ba-9d44-59089af5da16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6ec442b8-94e4-56aa-8807-ad0fa1f1a88b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d9c3e1ed-6dca-51d3-ad1c-1d7def1f515d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f26047d6-64fd-55d0-a8ff-9b0d3f1f2a6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d52f285-c0db-5927-ad1c-6a75afccabc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161866/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-161866/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161866/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161866/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161866/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-161866/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161866" :question/id #uuid "f0bc89ed-3046-579a-96f4-412ae4e827ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161866" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=1=f(-1).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1$ since ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=0=f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(1,3),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=2,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(2)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161866/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "9ae1a655-99be-5655-a3be-505d223f0a32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "fd173bfa-2606-5ec8-a9a8-f67b2d42062f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "042f64df-fc71-5aeb-870e-58020f56f1fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6223297b-3f1f-5765-a0be-fad3e5b45c5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "6ffd7393-b068-5965-ab30-48cd53a98bac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d97d62fd-b53d-5eea-a97f-b50e17a3172a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161845/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-161845/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-161845/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161845/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-161845/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161845/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161845" :question/id #uuid "1936251c-b682-5e21-bc78-b5e80d9892c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161845/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "dcb2816b-bc01-5a73-b318-d64cab1bbbbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8eece770-d44b-5537-bfbf-470cb187e0e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "82038214-af23-5d92-b605-c479872b7f67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "38d8610e-2763-5eab-b757-cdf505e84275" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "94c04578-6691-5740-b87f-bfc4e34d68b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "655e28ae-f320-5522-a2dd-7a0b4bf9b7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161857/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-161857/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161857/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-161857/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161857/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161857/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161857" :question/id #uuid "0bb25d39-9aff-5295-b9a5-be77270cb4ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161857/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161857"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e2535b4e-f3e6-55d4-b086-efea0bad6828" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e07f5743-1c94-583c-bc78-5c2b929a6aad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7e404ff8-d9cf-50ab-89d9-e6193a39ec62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8ac17f11-f929-58fa-8b29-6c363aa3e029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "7ee12a02-d879-59da-aec7-ea2d6571bb48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa57363-df1b-58f2-837f-51d51b554ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161865/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-161865/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161865/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161865/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-161865/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-161865/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161865" :question/id #uuid "0d66e1b1-7b49-597f-a208-7098855dadc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161865" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0.5,1.5),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-0.88=f(0),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is also false. Notice that $y=2.5=f(3).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=3$ since ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161865/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161865"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "d2570335-64e0-5b4e-82ce-b8e5464c0e31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "0920a172-bebe-563b-8068-37ef6b1c26a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8821660d-3b41-5a3c-abfa-c01dd286b01f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f13b29a6-0267-5e68-a25e-d6816fae062e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "89121b65-a753-58fb-bee5-239bc2c009a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "10cb6b95-c8b6-5ea3-a13a-c33055d49cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161858/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-161858/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-161858/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-161858/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161858/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161858/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161858" :question/id #uuid "7f944412-33cb-5081-bdb5-c7764378e69c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161858" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161858/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" :answer/id #uuid "b719b318-d302-5052-9a9f-2c04a697bc02" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" :answer/id #uuid "6ed34daf-85ff-56d1-89ed-98efc0952065" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection/4d5308f6880d88fc4e0c1e5cfe89e02fdd53558b504aa624fcb8d4e3392c00ed" :answer/id #uuid "262fba5d-ac5e-54bd-968b-1442aa1e7b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection/66d0b61a499d72470b5527092e737a7ab79701eaf7d8286f76e2c67906fd06bd" :answer/id #uuid "04c879d5-b9ae-5665-a4e5-f3829be5bafb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection/b7d3610105ac683c44f86bdf29d5b9ef389f6408ec7059df003e402d83c5bc23" :answer/id #uuid "03228b9f-7eaa-5a7d-b545-1c7206e99a35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b35531-e501-5ca1-a4e9-fb38af098a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160460/selection/4d5308f6880d88fc4e0c1e5cfe89e02fdd53558b504aa624fcb8d4e3392c00ed" :answer-field/choices ["q-160460/selection/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" "q-160460/selection/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" "q-160460/selection/4d5308f6880d88fc4e0c1e5cfe89e02fdd53558b504aa624fcb8d4e3392c00ed" "q-160460/selection/66d0b61a499d72470b5527092e737a7ab79701eaf7d8286f76e2c67906fd06bd" "q-160460/selection/b7d3610105ac683c44f86bdf29d5b9ef389f6408ec7059df003e402d83c5bc23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160460" :question/id #uuid "b086e929-2933-5923-87f3-a40760986e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection/ab63356239e33d80c4882ec5be3dd5ed56752b67af5174b433b1b3d857f97e58" :answer/id #uuid "9547cc08-919a-581e-a488-091512d8d9e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection/db461dec809acacd1d9eed8256b39bc67ffb2406794a2c89bc30a491c2461de6" :answer/id #uuid "84b7a5cc-cfbf-5eec-b766-bc9d8469fa1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection/8f386933185d00fbff379b5664c988026c52ffb188eee3e8fa91182ebdb5d28a" :answer/id #uuid "55cb0a30-0412-5433-8065-e70b4846928c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection/fd37b031ab84bfb8ec8eb3bb90ac8fcf4bf0d2a39fa13a91238360a7ae98a273" :answer/id #uuid "2e87d794-a38b-5848-84d8-7fd5ed580eae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection/159049bbdc812c6c7d3f5e556d3556d018fdd397f09b829c6d1d5633329f8f23" :answer/id #uuid "0a85f0c9-440a-5e46-a1a3-ed53a4984c78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f676447-132c-5db6-8347-2b9a917c9ceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160473/selection/fd37b031ab84bfb8ec8eb3bb90ac8fcf4bf0d2a39fa13a91238360a7ae98a273" :answer-field/choices ["q-160473/selection/ab63356239e33d80c4882ec5be3dd5ed56752b67af5174b433b1b3d857f97e58" "q-160473/selection/db461dec809acacd1d9eed8256b39bc67ffb2406794a2c89bc30a491c2461de6" "q-160473/selection/8f386933185d00fbff379b5664c988026c52ffb188eee3e8fa91182ebdb5d28a" "q-160473/selection/fd37b031ab84bfb8ec8eb3bb90ac8fcf4bf0d2a39fa13a91238360a7ae98a273" "q-160473/selection/159049bbdc812c6c7d3f5e556d3556d018fdd397f09b829c6d1d5633329f8f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160473" :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160473/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160473"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection/edaf235239b96ddb53a67fe7c0500708f303a996da95768bf48647c85758c857" :answer/id #uuid "56551ca8-13bc-5452-848d-039a8c0d943c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $uv=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection/ebe20e7f8738e5f5f45887ce5ff21a8b7dcbdbfc21029c5397f504113e492ce3" :answer/id #uuid "b623dfb1-f71e-588f-b887-aec5ef8b7d72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection/994ba792582e9f0cd98abb47e2eb2e62fee06c608b6f1b9ad3d62ee92e09851a" :answer/id #uuid "2d554aa2-a151-5249-9902-b06b8467a6f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection/8059caa1952472cbe72148f0c089a91bf45f670f7d986d9372c6281cf325ea05" :answer/id #uuid "c9a795de-9ba5-5977-8acf-f0c059ac74ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection/15290b35d7cf57abc907c60e8c16bb92ce90e85a601831d2e5c594eb04bf2433" :answer/id #uuid "557ad530-b79f-5d43-9d3b-3beaa66d4fce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4030c9c3-32ab-5c33-9366-129b0d91d1e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81274/selection/ebe20e7f8738e5f5f45887ce5ff21a8b7dcbdbfc21029c5397f504113e492ce3" :answer-field/choices ["q-81274/selection/edaf235239b96ddb53a67fe7c0500708f303a996da95768bf48647c85758c857" "q-81274/selection/ebe20e7f8738e5f5f45887ce5ff21a8b7dcbdbfc21029c5397f504113e492ce3" "q-81274/selection/994ba792582e9f0cd98abb47e2eb2e62fee06c608b6f1b9ad3d62ee92e09851a" "q-81274/selection/8059caa1952472cbe72148f0c089a91bf45f670f7d986d9372c6281cf325ea05" "q-81274/selection/15290b35d7cf57abc907c60e8c16bb92ce90e85a601831d2e5c594eb04bf2433"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81274" :question/id #uuid "08b26d40-c5f1-558a-a1d7-4ddf15b255a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81274" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{uv}$ and $y(u,v)=u+2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v{e}^{uv} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u{e}^{uv} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot 2-u{e}^{uv}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(2v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{uv}(2v-u) & =0 \\\\ 2v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe equation $u=2v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81274/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81274"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection/adeecce912cbdf3b61dd7afe3a9fd59c67f91c92c0c622115bc563b7268ebc3f" :answer/id #uuid "0c35f47a-8ebc-57fa-9c24-5e64620a4a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection/3261ee82c2b5581da4340d460c2611ac9289dc5134c041c8d83e4c227fed2fcf" :answer/id #uuid "194e4843-dc33-5afd-bdc7-3ac1c1ef2ea1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection/93986b1fd08e989adecc2968e07327cfd18082f8e739b78e8923a9d6f6e81cbf" :answer/id #uuid "b2687845-7270-5e6b-bf80-18af3c3faf92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection/ebe20e7f8738e5f5f45887ce5ff21a8b7dcbdbfc21029c5397f504113e492ce3" :answer/id #uuid "07e59a61-c9e7-5b88-94a0-151cf98656dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection/cd181b8ab9e67bb0653a6fe7e9ce90524748b947de8b1556339c5edc762eae6a" :answer/id #uuid "46631a05-f41c-598d-8043-806b1f53599c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b94ee39-c4fb-5827-a1f0-aa4a945374d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160477/selection/3261ee82c2b5581da4340d460c2611ac9289dc5134c041c8d83e4c227fed2fcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-160477/selection/adeecce912cbdf3b61dd7afe3a9fd59c67f91c92c0c622115bc563b7268ebc3f" "q-160477/selection/3261ee82c2b5581da4340d460c2611ac9289dc5134c041c8d83e4c227fed2fcf" "q-160477/selection/93986b1fd08e989adecc2968e07327cfd18082f8e739b78e8923a9d6f6e81cbf" "q-160477/selection/ebe20e7f8738e5f5f45887ce5ff21a8b7dcbdbfc21029c5397f504113e492ce3" "q-160477/selection/cd181b8ab9e67bb0653a6fe7e9ce90524748b947de8b1556339c5edc762eae6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160477" :question/id #uuid "9f0fb416-ba54-5520-a3cd-23b891dc09c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-160477" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160477/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160477"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186198928 :question/answer-fields 17592186197803] {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" :answer/id #uuid "66cd5471-a3a9-5b0f-bd01-a85a8ddea804" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/aebe5dec80a3dee8c7aa602ead68f49eb7f90207e6c54f86bb517351699ceb6e" :answer/id #uuid "eb844b1f-c4e4-5fd2-8cf2-d8b2ea7f2da2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/ec213c0f6ddca4b1f1eecc4708609f0e7d49660e64c66df1f0010f88433c9f20" :answer/id #uuid "052efdc3-ad52-5311-be2c-b97b25e083a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" :answer/id #uuid "e80b04c9-a1ac-577a-811d-8ce910e2fd9a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/1eb1804da18743b97431502562dd001769c020cc36cc99120254af18d7266d4c" :answer/id #uuid "1640e8b4-b2ab-5e2f-91cb-8ef692109cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7191d8e-2bb6-54be-9067-def21eae756d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/aebe5dec80a3dee8c7aa602ead68f49eb7f90207e6c54f86bb517351699ceb6e" :answer-field/choices ["q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/aebe5dec80a3dee8c7aa602ead68f49eb7f90207e6c54f86bb517351699ceb6e" "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/ec213c0f6ddca4b1f1eecc4708609f0e7d49660e64c66df1f0010f88433c9f20" "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" "q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232/1eb1804da18743b97431502562dd001769c020cc36cc99120254af18d7266d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-81169"] :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81169/selection/ma-v1/525d76b9902e9b625240ed3d2fd9d9a59092c2a99463754b8ba9088bbf52b232"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81333/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "e8fc056f-3ac3-54ec-bc57-4220e1dc20f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81333/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "a5d5a325-cc43-542a-8a42-5a21e54e484e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81333/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answe