ned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160563/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160563"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "96bc3664-62f5-58c7-9359-a5e0bddaf40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection/067f8394ee42d8135073fd0c196cd07b15b62bf00b668a6dd30a825b7176ad3f" :answer/id #uuid "84194ed3-152e-51ef-9fed-4d26e5b97dba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "d85a72eb-8030-543e-884a-73d01b31b02f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "9c59a974-ba57-5f3c-af4c-5070b0609f93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "ee2f2ebf-ed3f-5d89-be74-95d5dbc33054" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2530e453-37b5-5e8b-a1d1-14494d277462" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160581/selection/067f8394ee42d8135073fd0c196cd07b15b62bf00b668a6dd30a825b7176ad3f" :answer-field/choices ["q-160581/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-160581/selection/067f8394ee42d8135073fd0c196cd07b15b62bf00b668a6dd30a825b7176ad3f" "q-160581/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-160581/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" "q-160581/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160581" :question/id #uuid "9e6a6139-c56b-5ec6-8b4e-5f56e91ad2e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160581" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160581/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160581"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-81305"] :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "9a5150d7-1e56-52ae-8b81-8b7fbcda71d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "8a05c7ef-16f1-5d39-b61c-b653c9107f87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" :answer/id #uuid "dc69252c-a54e-5d0d-9fa1-5c5c9e8dea28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection/8894e02db0908440805a1806bb00c8249730e10177d5fa80a64cfac4e99b6663" :answer/id #uuid "5af6ec9d-299c-5fef-9cc2-8cd4d46b28c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e9f5ebb5-db7f-5b89-af73-e842139f0470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01676cf1-bde2-5293-a4a0-fcec2cf155a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81370/selection/8894e02db0908440805a1806bb00c8249730e10177d5fa80a64cfac4e99b6663" :answer-field/choices ["q-81370/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-81370/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-81370/selection/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" "q-81370/selection/8894e02db0908440805a1806bb00c8249730e10177d5fa80a64cfac4e99b6663" "q-81370/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81370" :question/id #uuid "49fc0404-bc55-50c7-a26f-a9bc9a4c5efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-81370" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-4y \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=5x-4y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =5 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81370/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81370"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12416"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\n- $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\n- The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7127"] :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-11157"] :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/149438cd70059230993ca93a2122158f71edb665b3948cbd4ff3a9c038d7e48f.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
